    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-kingship">sacred kingship</a> in the hills east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a>, whose dynasty tradition traces to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ntare-i-of-burundi">Ntare Rushatsi</a> around 1680.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ntare-iv-rugamba">Ntare IV Rugamba</a> roughly doubled its territory in the early 19th century, giving Burundi borders close to today’s.</li><li>It survived German and Belgian rule to become independent in 1962, but army officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-micombero">Michel Micombero</a> abolished it in 1966.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The mountain gorilla’s species, Gorilla beringei, is named after Robert von Beringe, the German officer who shot two of the apes in 1902 and in 1903 led the campaign that forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwezi-iv-gisabo">Mwezi IV Gisabo</a> to submit.</p></aside>
    </section>

